WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.320
The painting you are about to see is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:07.920
without a doubt, the most unsettling&nbsp;
self-portrait ever created in history.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.640
In a self-portrait, the artist decides&nbsp;
how they want to be seen by the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.120
They choose their best angle, their best&nbsp;
clothes, and paint themselves as they wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:22.560
to be remembered by history. And that is&nbsp;
precisely why this image is so disturbing.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:26.960
Because when Arnold Böcklin decided to&nbsp;
paint himself, at forty-five years old,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.200
at the peak of his career, he chose&nbsp;
to include something that no artist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.520
in their right mind would include in&nbsp;
the image that would immortalize them.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:41.280
Behind him, pressed against his back, Death&nbsp;
plays the violin directly into his ear.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.120
But the most terrifying thing about&nbsp;
this is not just the presence of Death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.040
but the small detail this artist painted on&nbsp;
the violin the skeleton holds. A detail that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.200
once you discover its meaning, will make you&nbsp;
see this work in a completely different way.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.320
This work is called Self-Portrait with Death&nbsp;
Playing the Fiddle. It was painted by the Swiss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:09.520
artist Arnold Böcklin in 1872, and today it&nbsp;
is held at the Alte Nationalgalerie in Berlin.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.560
In it we see Böcklin standing at his easel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.920
with a palette in one hand and a brush in&nbsp;
the other. His gaze is directed toward us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.200
or perhaps toward a mirror, because let&nbsp;
us remember that he is painting himself.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:28.320
Everything in the lower half of the painting is&nbsp;
normal. A painter in his studio, in the middle of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.000
his work. But from the upper half, emerging from&nbsp;
the darkness of the background, there is Death.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.080
But to understand why Böcklin&nbsp;
painted himself accompanied by Death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.000
we first need to know the&nbsp;
tragic life this man lived.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.400
Arnold Böcklin was born in Basel, Switzerland,&nbsp;
in 1827. And if there is one thing that defines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.320
his biography, it is that death accompanied&nbsp;
him from very early on and never left him.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.200
Böcklin had fourteen children throughout&nbsp;
his life. Five of them died in childhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:05.520
and three more would die before him. By&nbsp;
1872, the year he painted this self-portrait,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:11.360
he had already buried two of them. He also&nbsp;
survived cholera and typhus epidemics that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.320
devastated the cities where he lived, and&nbsp;
his own health was fragile for decades.

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.120
For most people, a life like that would&nbsp;
have been reason enough to hate death. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.520
Böcklin did exactly the opposite, and&nbsp;
turned it into the center of his art.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:32.000
His paintings are filled with cemeteries, funerary&nbsp;
islands, and spectral figures. Eight years after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.520
this self-portrait he would paint Isle of the&nbsp;
Dead, the work that would make him world famous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.831
and that would become one of the most haunting&nbsp;
images of the entire nineteenth century.

00:02:42.831 --> 00:02:42.880
But it all started here. With this self-portrait.

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:48.320
When we look at this painting, the first thing we&nbsp;
notice is the contrast between the two figures.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:54.080
Böcklin is painted in warm colors. His face&nbsp;
is full of life, his beard is well groomed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:59.360
and his clothes are those of a respectable&nbsp;
artist of his time. Death, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:04.240
emerges from a dark, cold background,&nbsp;
almost dissolving into the shadows.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.440
The second thing that catches our attention is&nbsp;
the artist's posture. Böcklin holds the brush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:15.040
and the palette, but if we look closely, he is&nbsp;
not painting. His hand is still, and his head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:21.280
tilts slightly backward, toward the violin. As if&nbsp;
his entire body were in an attitude of listening.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.280
And this is where we arrive at the&nbsp;
detail I mentioned at the beginning.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:31.120
When we get closer to the violin, we can see that&nbsp;
it is incomplete and missing strings. This tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.720
us that Death is not playing a full instrument,&nbsp;
but playing on a single string. The G string.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:43.040
The lowest of them all, and the one that produces&nbsp;
the deepest and darkest sound a violin can make.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.160
And here it is worth mentioning a curious&nbsp;
fact about this artist. Böcklin was known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.400
to have the habit of listening to&nbsp;
the strangest music while he painted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.520
It was part of his creative process. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:01.600
there is a story about this work that says the&nbsp;
self-portrait originally did not include Death.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.480
According to that account, a friend visited&nbsp;
his studio, saw the unfinished painting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.520
and asked Böcklin what he was listening to,&nbsp;
because of the distant expression in his eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.360
and the slight tilt of his head. And Böcklin's&nbsp;
response was to add the skeleton behind him.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.200
We do not know if that story is&nbsp;
completely true. But what we do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:26.400
know is that the decision to leave a single&nbsp;
string on that violin was no coincidence.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:32.480
Many historians interpret that single string as&nbsp;
a symbol of the passage of time. As if at birth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:36.320
the violin had all of its strings,&nbsp;
and over the years they broke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.440
one by one. Each broken string could&nbsp;
represent a piece of life left behind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:46.480
until only the last one remains.&nbsp;
The one playing the final melody.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.840
Something worth clarifying is that Böcklin&nbsp;
did not invent this iconography from scratch.

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:58.240
The image of Death as a musician comes from a&nbsp;
medieval tradition known as the Danse Macabre, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:04.320
the Dance of Death. After the great plagues that&nbsp;
devastated Europe, artists began depicting Death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:10.640
as a figure that dances and plays music, taking&nbsp;
kings, bishops, peasants, and children alike.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.560
The message these images conveyed&nbsp;
was simple and democratic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:19.040
as they tried to communicate to the&nbsp;
world that death does not discriminate.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.080
But there is one specific work that historians&nbsp;
point to as the direct precedent for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:30.640
self-portrait. A sixteenth-century portrait of&nbsp;
Sir Brian Tuke, attributed to the circle of Hans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.560
Holbein, in which the figure of Death appears&nbsp;
behind the sitter pointing at an hourglass.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.040
It is quite possible that Böcklin knew&nbsp;
this painting, since by the time he painted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.800
his self-portrait, this work was already in&nbsp;
Munich, the same city where he created his own.

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:49.680
But although he may have drawn&nbsp;
inspiration from that work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:55.360
Böcklin made a fundamental change. In Tuke's&nbsp;
portrait, Death points to the time running out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:02.080
while in Böcklin's, Death makes music. And the&nbsp;
artist, instead of being terrified, listens.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.840
For centuries, the memento mori tradition&nbsp;
used the image of death to provoke fear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:13.440
To remind us that we will die and that we&nbsp;
must be prepared for that moment. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.960
Böcklin turned that tradition&nbsp;
completely on its head.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:22.560
In this self-portrait, Death should not be&nbsp;
understood as a threat. On the contrary, it is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:29.280
muse. The artist is not being pursued, but rather&nbsp;
being inspired by the melody playing in his ear.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:32.240
And when we consider the life this artist lived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.840
it all begins to make sense. That constant&nbsp;
awareness of mortality, shaped by the loss of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:44.000
his children and the epidemics he survived,&nbsp;
may have been exactly what fueled his art.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:50.080
This self-portrait was exhibited in Munich in&nbsp;
1872, and from the moment it was shown to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:56.554
public, it established Böcklin's reputation in the&nbsp;
German art world. Today, more than one hundred and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.554 --> 00:07:02.160
fifty years later, it is still considered one of&nbsp;
the most unsettling self-portraits ever painted.

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.200
Not because of the skeleton, but because of&nbsp;
what it tells us about the man who painted it.

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:12.320
And perhaps that is the true question this work&nbsp;
leaves us with. Because each of us hears that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.320 --> 00:07:17.760
melody in a different way. There are those who&nbsp;
see death as an inevitable punishment that all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.360
life is destined for. There are those who see it&nbsp;
as a farewell they do not wish to give. And there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:29.120
are those who see it as a liberation, a way out&nbsp;
of all the suffering that life has caused them.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.000
Böcklin chose to listen to it with serenity. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:35.120
would love to know in the&nbsp;
comments how you hear it.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.600
Thank you for watching the video until the&nbsp;
end. As always, I would like to remind you&nbsp;&nbsp;

